  "title": "Harmel, Epilepsy and Protection from Jinn",
  "data": "The Holy Prophet (s.a.w.a.) is reported to have said, “For every tree, leaf and seed of harmel (also harmala or wild rue, a plant with aromatic seeds used locally for medicines, oil and dye), there is an angel entrusted with it until it withers. In the root and branches of this plant is a cure for distress and sorcery, and in its seeds there is a remedy for seventy-two maladies. So treat yourselves with this plant and with frankincense.”\nEpilepsy\nIt is reported that when Imam al-Reza (a.s.) once passed by an epileptic person, he asked for a cup of water to be brought to him. The Imam (a.s.) recited [Surah al-Fatihah](A5), [Surah al-Falaq](A117) and [Surah al-Nas](A118) over the cup and blew into it. He then told them to pour that water on the head and face of the epileptic person. Immediately, the man regained consciousness, and the Imam (a.s.) said to him, “This will never afflict you again.”\nRepelling the Evils of Jinn\nThe Holy Prophet (s.a.w.a.) is reported to have said: Whoever is pelted by a jinn with a stone may pick up the stone, throw it back in the direction from which it came, and say:\nحَسْبِىَ اللّٰهُ وَكَفٰى وَسَمِعَ اللّٰهُ لِمَنْ دَعَا لَيْسَ وَرَاۤءَ اللّٰهِ مُنْتَهٰى\nHASBIYALLAAHU WA KAFAA WA SAMI'ALLAAHU LIMAN DA'AA LAYSA WARAA'ALLAAHI MUNTAHAA\nAllah is sufficient for me, and He suffices. Allah hears whoever calls upon Him. Beyond Allah there is no end.\nTo keep oneself safe from jinn, it is useful to keep chickens, roosters and young goats in one's house.\nTo keep oneself safe from jinn on journeys, in wastelands and in places thought to be haunted, Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) is reported to have advised that one should put his hand on the middle of his head and recite the following holy verse in the lowest voice:\nاَفَغَيْرَ دِيْنِ اللّٰهِ يَبْغُوْنَ وَلَهٗ اَسْلَمَ مَنْ فِى السَّمٰوَاتِ وَالْاَرْضِ طَوْعًا وَكَرْهًا وَّاِلَيْهِ يُرْجَعُوْنَ\nAFAGHAYRA DEENILLAAHI YABGHOONA WA LAHOO ASLAMA MAN FIS SAMAAWAATI WAL ARDI TAW'AN WA KARHAN WA ILAYHI YURJA'OON\nIs it then other than Allah's religion that they seek, while to Him submits whoever is in the heavens and the earth, willingly or unwillingly, and to Him shall they be returned? (3:83)\nIt is also reported that, on entering a place thought to be haunted, it is advisable to say the adhan."
}
